WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Good morning, Hank.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.110
It's Tuesday.

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:05.620
I want to talk today about Boko Haram which
was formed in Nigeria in 2002, but I want

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.270
to begin in the year 1100-ish.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:13.049
So Hank, we have this idea that Islam spread
primarily through conquest, but in fact, in

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:18.000
many places, including Sub-Saharan Africa,
Islam spread primarily by trade.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.260
So back in the year 1100-ish, leaders in what
is now Northern Nigeria started to convert

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:23.260
to Islam.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.950
They were trading with North African Berbers
and by the 18th century, most of the people

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:31.530
in what is now Northern Nigeria and also what
is now Southwestern Nigeria were Muslims.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:35.340
But those Sub-Saharan African empires never
spread to the rest of Southern Nigeria so

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.090
those people were not Muslims.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:40.540
Instead, in the 19th century, when a bunch
of European missionaries showed up, many of

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.590
those people in the rest of Southern Nigeria
became Christians.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:47.380
What is now Nigeria was colonized by the British
for much of the 20th century, but Northern

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:51.239
Nigeria was controlled by what's known as
indirect rule.

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:54.829
Like the local emirs were allowed to run everything
as long as they pledged allegiance to the

00:00:54.829 --> 00:00:57.360
British and also, paid them a portion of their
money.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.990
Now, as far as colonization goes, that seems
like a pretty good deal, but also meant there

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:05.770
was much less western-style education in Northern
Nigeria than in Southern Nigeria which was

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.880
much more directly ruled by the British.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.300
So the south ended up with most of the doctors
and the lawyers and the bureaucrats.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.770
Also, all of the oil is in the south.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.970
I should pause here to say that this is of
course over simplifying.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:20.040
There are hundreds of languages spoken in
Nigeria and hundreds of different ethnic groups.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.270
There are many Christians living in predominantly
Muslim communities; many Muslims living in

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:24.479
predominantly Christian communities.

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:28.330
So it's definitely not as simple as a divide
between North and South, but there is a big

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:29.330
gap there.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:33.280
Ok Hank, so one of the many catastrophes of
colonization is that it created borders that

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.270
were convenient to Europeans, but not necessarily
to the people who, like, lived in the actual

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.270
places.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:43.549
So in 1960, Nigeria gains independence which
is awesome except it's never actually been,

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:45.479
like, a unified political entity.

00:01:45.479 --> 00:01:48.850
Hank, there's this famous moment in James
Joyce's novel, "Ulysses" where Leopold Bloom

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:53.190
is asked what a nation is, and Bloom answers,
"A nation is the same people living in the

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.299
same place - or also living in different places."

00:01:56.299 --> 00:02:00.390
But 1960, Nigeria was more like different
people living in the same place which led

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:04.130
to a bunch of coups and then, eventually,
to a 1967 civil war.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:07.270
Parts of Southern Nigeria seceded and became
the Republic of Biafra.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.060
And the rest of Nigeria was like, "Wait, no.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.259
We should be one Nigeria.

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:12.540
Also, all the oil is in your new country."

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.630
There was a huge civil war for two and a half
years.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:18.640
More than a million people died, but in the
end, Nigeria won, and the Republic of Biafra

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.080
ceased to exist, and the country was reunited.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.090
So Nigeria today is a really interesting country.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.220
It's split almost evenly between Christians
and Muslims.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.810
It has one of the fastest growing economies
in the world and very little national debt.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:32.120
It's the largest economy in Africa and the
20th largest in the world.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.640
Their film industry generates 10 billion dollars
in sales a year.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.220
But it's also the last nation in Africa that
still has polio, which is found only in the

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:43.140
north, and life expectancy in Nigeria is 52.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.489
That's lower than it is in Somalia which doesn't
even have a government.

00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:50.580
In much of Northern Nigeria, fewer than half
of kids even go to elementary school.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.580
Female literacy is over 50% and while the
country's overall economy grows over 8% a

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:59.099
year, in the north, absolute poverty is actually
increasing.

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:02.200
Hank, it's easy enough to say that religion
is the problem here, right?

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:06.099
Except there are a lot of Muslims living in
the southwest of Nigeria where female literacy

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:09.400
is over 90% and the economy is growing really
well.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.250
The problem is partly resource allegation.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:15.879
Like, Nigeria's export economy is just a smidge
dependent upon petroleum which is found almost

00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:17.090
exclusively in the south.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:21.160
But there's also all these legacies of colonialism
and indirect rule which means less effective

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.700
federal governance in everything from education
to the armed forces.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:30.239
So this was the Northern Nigeria that saw
Boko Haram emerge in 2002, a region disproportionately

00:03:30.239 --> 00:03:32.800
impoverished, uneducated, and poorly governed.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.020
The words Boko Haram literally mean "western
education is forbidden".

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:41.540
The group was founded by a guy named Mohammed
Yusuf who himself had a western-style education

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:42.590
and spoke English.

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:46.490
Although he believed that the earth is flat
and that rain is not caused by evaporation,

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:48.390
so maybe it wasn't such a great education.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:53.170
In 2002, he began recruiting unemployed young
people into his movement, railing against

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:58.080
official corruption, which is rampant, and
also advocating for the creation of Islamic

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.080
state.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:02.989
But Boko Haram didn't actually become a military
organization until Yusuf was arrested and

00:04:02.989 --> 00:04:05.970
then shortly thereafter, executed in 2009.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:09.489
Over a thousand people were killed in the
riots that followed Mohammed Yusuf's execution,

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:13.040
and after that, Boko Haram became increasingly
militant and crazy.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.159
I mean, this is an organization that claims
they want to restore like righteousness and

00:04:17.159 --> 00:04:21.719
justice to Nigeria, but they fund themselves
like primarily through drug trafficking and

00:04:21.719 --> 00:04:22.719
bank robbery.

00:04:22.719 --> 00:04:27.050
In 2011, Boko Haram was behind Nigeria's first
ever suicide bombing.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:31.749
On Christmas of that year, they killed 41
people via bombings and shootings in churches.

00:04:31.749 --> 00:04:35.050
In 2012, they killed at least 792 people.

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:39.819
And in 2013, they murdered nine women who
were distributing polio vaccines.

00:04:39.819 --> 00:04:40.819
Polio vaccines!

00:04:40.819 --> 00:04:44.990
They also murdered 54 mostly Muslim college
students while they were sleeping in their

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:45.990
dorms.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:50.729
And then, in 2014, Boko Haram got worldwide
attention when they kidnapped 276 girls from

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:52.009
inside of classrooms.

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:54.180
The attacks since then have only gotten worse.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.650
On January 3rd of this year, they seized the
town of Baga, burned it to the ground, murdered

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:00.580
as many as 2000 people.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.419
And now, Boko Haram controls the northeastern
corner of Nigeria where they've instituted

00:05:04.419 --> 00:05:09.150
a version of sharia law so extreme that not
only has the likes of it never been seen in

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:12.479
Sub-Saharan Africa, but it's, like, too extreme
for al-Qaeda.

00:05:12.479 --> 00:05:16.620
They regularly massacre people who won't fight
for them, and they've begun attacks in Niger

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.250
and Chad and Cameroon.

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:23.979
Boko Haram still has an army between 7,000-10,000
people, and it's difficult to understand what

00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:26.930
those people are thinking, at least what the
leaders are thinking.

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:31.149
I mean, they conscript a lot of people; they
have a lot of child soldiers; they force preteen

00:05:31.149 --> 00:05:32.689
girls to be suicide bombers.

00:05:32.689 --> 00:05:35.249
And it's hard to understand that evil.

00:05:35.249 --> 00:05:39.340
Then again, I don't know if I need to understand
it, but I do need to know about it.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:42.789
I mean, not knowing about this stuff is really
problematic, Hank, because one of the great

00:05:42.789 --> 00:05:46.629
tragedies of Boko Haram is that they have
been allowed to grow for the last ten years

00:05:46.629 --> 00:05:50.770
because the Nigerian government and the world
community haven't done a good enough job of

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:53.139
noticing how terrible they are.

00:05:53.139 --> 00:05:56.650
So I think it's really important to learn
about Boko Haram and not let their atrocities

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:03.099
go unnoticed, but I also think it's important
to understand that literally 99.9% of Nigerians

00:06:03.099 --> 00:06:04.750
do not support Boko Haram.

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:08.550
And Hank, it's also important to remember
that just a few days ago, two Muslim volunteers

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:12.929
in a northern town sacrificed their lives
to keep a Boko Haram suicide bomber from entering

00:06:12.929 --> 00:06:15.639
a city market where she would have killed
hundreds of people.

00:06:15.639 --> 00:06:16.689
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

